<td id="S5.T1.2.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TakeItBack-v0</span></td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.42<math id="S5.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.24</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.08<math id="S5.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.04</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.33<math id="S5.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.10</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.04<math id="S5.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.01</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb">0.05<math id="S5.T1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.02</td>
<td id="S5.T1.2.1.5.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5BBEA;"><span id="S5.T1.2.1.5.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5BBEA;">0.78<math id="S5.T1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#D5BBEA;" mathbackground="#D5BBEA">±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.03</span></td></tr>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Results</h3>

<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.07151v1/matl_heatmap.svg" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:274/137;" width="274" height="137"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Generalization of ELMUR across T-Maze lengths.</span>
Each cell shows success rate (mean <math id="S5.F4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> standard error) for training vs. validation lengths.
ELMUR transfers perfectly: models trained on shorter sequences retain 100% success up to <math id="S5.F4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9600" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9600</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9600</annotation></semantics></math> steps. Training lengths were split into three equal segments.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate ELMUR on T-Maze, MIKASA-Robo, and POPGym, addressing RQ1–RQ5 on retention, generalization, manipulation, cross-domain robustness, and ablations.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RQ1: Retention beyond attention.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To test memory retention, we train on T-Maze corridors of length <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> while restricting the context size to <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L&lt;T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L&lt;T</annotation></semantics></math>, forcing the model to solve tasks where the cue must be preserved beyond the native attention span. At validation, we evaluate on much longer</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">corridors — up to one million steps — without increasing <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby probing memory retention far beyond the training horizon. ELMUR achieves <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> success even under this extreme extrapolation (<a href="#S5.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Training Details and Hardware. ‣ 5.1 Benchmarks and Baselines ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ ELMUR: External Layer Memory with Update/Rewrite for Long-Horizon RL" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 3</span></a>), implying retention horizons nearly <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="100{,}000{\times}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100,000</mn><mo lspace="0.222em">×</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100{,}000{\times}</annotation></semantics></math> larger than the attention window (<math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L{=}10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L{=}10</annotation></semantics></math> with only <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S{=}3</annotation></semantics></math> segments used during training).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RQ2: Generalization across sequence lengths.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We train ELMUR on T-Maze with short contexts (3 to 300 steps) and then evaluate across 11 validation lengths ranging from 9 to 9600 steps. The model transfers seamlessly in both directions: it solves tasks shorter than those seen during training without overfitting to a fixed scale, and it also extrapolates to sequences orders of magnitude longer. As shown in <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.2 Results ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ ELMUR: External Layer Memory with Update/Rewrite for Long-Horizon RL" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure 4</span></a>, ELMUR maintains <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> success across all train/test pairs, demonstrating robust generalization beyond the training horizon.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RQ3: Manipulation with visual observations.</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Results in <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Training Details and Hardware. ‣ 5.1 Benchmarks and Baselines ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ ELMUR: External Layer Memory with Update/Rewrite for Long-Horizon RL" class="ltx_ref ltx_refmacro_autoref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table 1</span></a> indicate that ELMUR achieves higher success rates than other baselines on the MIKASA-Robo tasks. In <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">TakeItBack-v0</span>, it obtains <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.78{\pm}0.03" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.78</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.03</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.78{\pm}0.03</annotation></semantics></math> compared to <math id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.42{\pm}0.24" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.42</mn><mo>±</mo><mn>0.24</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.42{\pm}0.24</annotation></semantics></math> for the next-best model, and in <span id="S5.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">RememberColor[3,5,9]-v0</span> its performance remains stable as the number of distractors increases. Overall, ELMUR shows more reliable performance under visual interference in manipulation tasks with pixel inputs.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.07151v1/matl_vs_dt_symlog_circles.svg" id="S5.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/119;" width="548" height="119"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 5: </span>
ELMUR compared to DT on all 48 POPGym tasks. Each model was trained with three independent runs, validated over 100 episodes each. Bars show the mean performance with 95% confidence intervals computed over these three means. ELMUR achieves consistent improvements over DT, with the largest gains on memory-intensive puzzles.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:52%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Aggregated returns on 48 POPGym tasks.</figcaption>
